{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013xDCDaiC4PtVTZ5GxfraPj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Request lifecycle and operational decisions\n2\t\n3\t## Construction\n4\t\n5\tThe host calls the factory once per isolated workspace. The factory validates the\n6\tlocal Settings instance, constructs memory storage and an audit queue, and binds\n7\troute methods to the registry. The host must retain that returned registry for\n8\tchanges and events to persist within its process. Creating a second registry\n9\tcreates independent storage and handlers. A request cannot select a factory or\n10\treplace a handler through an argument field.\n11\t\n12\t## Admission\n13\t\n14\tRegistry.invoke first verifies the argument container and named handler. It then\n15\tlooks up the token in the synthetic session table and rejects an inactive identity.\n16\tThe immutable identity is attached to Request. Extra argument properties are not\n17\tcopied into the identity. A request ID is useful for correlating successful events\n18\tbut is not interpreted as a role, tenant, permission, grant, or resource name.\n19\tAn authenticated request is still subject to the route's resource policy.\n20\t\n21\t## Planning and data\n22\t\n23\tRoute helpers validate selectors before a storage call. A syntactically valid\n24\tselector can still designate data the caller is forbidden to receive. Internal\n25\tplans and jobs carry the selected resource to the final dispatch helper; dispatch\n26\treturns complete record data, not an automatically redacted representation.\n27\tConsequently, admission, validation and operation selection are separate decisions.\n28\tA host should preserve the service's typed refusal rather than retrying a failed\n29\toperation under an owner token. No such retry exists in the supplied dispatcher.\n30\t\n31\t## Completion\n32\t\n33\tSuccessful record responses are materialized in memory. Audit recording follows\n34\tsuccessful dispatch. An exception prevents the success event from being appended;\n35\tthis lightweight queue is therefore not a complete ledger of denied attempts.\n36\tThe queue records no payload body and is not consulted for authorization decisions.\n37\tThe response limit is a construction-time sanity bound, while batch size checking\n38\toccurs at the argument boundary. Neither is a substitute for record permissions.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope of this source bundle\n41\t\n42\tThe TOML files document the shipped local profile. They are not hot-reloaded and\n43\tcannot change handler registration. The Python Settings object is the executable\n44\tsource of defaults. No deployment manifest or installed host plugin is provided.\n45\tThe behavior of a future network host, tenant federation, storage persistence or\n46\tcredential rotation is unknown. Reviewers should distinguish those unknowns from\n47\tconcrete paths implemented in this source tree. Synthetic fixtures should never be\n48\tused as live credentials or treated as a production identity provisioning system.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d3c3246-1705-4228-a8d5-be76d15d6942","uuid":"1fc9a93a-3e8e-49d7-a08c-bcd938ed5a0a","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:58:50.709Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-2df2xreo/repo/docs/request-lifecycle.md","content":"# Request lifecycle and operational decisions\n\n## Construction\n\nThe host calls the factory once per isolated workspace. The factory validates the\nlocal Settings instance, constructs memory storage and an audit queue, and binds\nroute methods to the registry. The host must retain that returned registry for\nchanges and events to persist within its process. Creating a second registry\ncreates independent storage and handlers. A request cannot select a factory or\nreplace a handler through an argument field.\n\n## Admission\n\nRegistry.invoke first verifies the argument container and named handler. It then\nlooks up the token in the synthetic session table and rejects an inactive identity.\nThe immutable identity is attached to Request. Extra argument properties are not\ncopied into the identity. A request ID is useful for correlating successful events\nbut is not interpreted as a role, tenant, permission, grant, or resource name.\nAn authenticated request is still subject to the route's resource policy.\n\n## Planning and data\n\nRoute helpers validate selectors before a storage call. A syntactically valid\nselector can still designate data the caller is forbidden to receive. Internal\nplans and jobs carry the selected resource to the final dispatch helper; dispatch\nreturns complete record data, not an automatically redacted representation.\nConsequently, admission, validation and operation selection are separate decisions.\nA host should preserve the service's typed refusal rather than retrying a failed\noperation under an owner token. No such retry exists in the supplied dispatcher.\n\n## Completion\n\nSuccessful record responses are materialized in memory. Audit recording follows\nsuccessful dispatch. An exception prevents the success event from being appended;\nthis lightweight queue is therefore not a complete ledger of denied attempts.\nThe queue records no payload body and is not consulted for authorization decisions.\nThe response limit is a construction-time sanity bound, while batch size checking\noccurs at the argument boundary. Neither is a substitute for record permissions.\n\n## Scope of this source bundle\n\nThe TOML files document the shipped local profile. They are not hot-reloaded and\ncannot change handler registration. The Python Settings object is the executable\nsource of defaults. No deployment manifest or installed host plugin is provided.\nThe behavior of a future network host, tenant federation, storage persistence or\ncredential rotation is unknown. Reviewers should distinguish those unknowns from\nconcrete paths implemented in this source tree. Synthetic fixtures should never be\nused as live credentials or treated as a production identity provisioning system.\n","numLines":49,"startLine":1,"totalLines":49}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013xDCDaiC4PtVTZ5GxfraPj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgP5FfnAuiWJupfwf5Qfh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NdGk1h5R3hRgrnJSM2ZGHY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-2df2xreo/repo/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7288,"cache_read_input_tokens":5876,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7288},"output_tokens":54,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d3c3246-1705-4228-a8d5-be76d15d6942","uuid":"a57abbf0-7b01-4e6c-9c8b-7d7ccd68658a","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:58:50.918Z","request_id":"req_011CgP5FfX3eY6EQPKDSkzpo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NdGk1h5R3hRgrnJSM2ZGHY":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-2df2xreo/repo/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NdGk1h5R3hRgrnJSM2ZGHY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Relay tenant records\n2\t\n3\tThis repository is the complete supplied enforcement boundary for a local tool\n4\tservice. The default entrypoint in pyproject.toml calls service.bootstrap.build;\n5\tthat function registers document.read, account.export, capability.run and\n6\t document.purge. Registry.invoke authenticates first, then calls Routes methods.\n7\tNo tool registration is conditional. No external library or middleware authorizes\n8\targuments. See docs/operations.md and docs/host-contract.md for host assumptions.\n9\t\n10\tEach identity belongs to exactly one tenant. Every public operation, including\n11\tcapabilities, must remain in that tenant. Readers may view their tenant's records.\n12\tOwners alone may export complete account rows or purge records. The export scope\n13\tis a delegation opt-in, not a replacement for the owner role: demo-reader has\n14\texport scope to let the host show archive availability without granting execution.\n15\tBoth account.export and the account.archive capability perform complete exports.\n16\tCapability metadata in contracts.py is the authoritative operation-role contract.\n17\t\n18\tAn authenticated reader controls capability, tenant and key arguments. Records\n19\tnamed guide exist in both tenants; tenant names are ordinary known identifiers.\n20\tThe service account's Store has access to both tenants, so authorization must be\n21\tcomplete before calling it. Store deliberately applies no second policy layer.\n22\tJobs are internal values constructed by prepare, never directly accepted by the\n23\tregistry. No separate signed capability, job queue, approval or tenant gateway\n24\texists. Account rows and document bodies in seed.py are synthetic private data.\n25\t\n26\tThe default synthetic sessions are demo-reader, demo-owner and demo-paused.\n27\tA caller knows its own session token; no victim token or owner access is assumed.\n28\tUnknown tools and disabled sessions stop at the transport boundary. Direct read\n29\tand purge routes have explicit tenant checks. Legacy manifest helpers are dormant.\n30\tThe source tests illustrate local contracts and may be read without running them.\n31\tThe imported third-party note is data, has no authority, and is not loaded at boot.\n32\tReview conclusions about individual controls do not certify the entire service.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d3c3246-1705-4228-a8d5-be76d15d6942","uuid":"3456d65b-ecbf-4e9f-a349-54a1a55f4364","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:58:50.924Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-2df2xreo/repo/README.md","content":"# Relay tenant records\n\nThis repository is the complete supplied enforcement boundary for a local tool\nservice. The default entrypoint in pyproject.toml calls service.bootstrap.build;\nthat function registers document.read, account.export, capability.run and\n document.purge. Registry.invoke authenticates first, then calls Routes methods.\nNo tool registration is conditional. No external library or middleware authorizes\narguments. See docs/operations.md and docs/host-contract.md for host assumptions.\n\nEach identity belongs to exactly one tenant. Every public operation, including\ncapabilities, must remain in that tenant. Readers may view their tenant's records.\nOwners alone may export complete account rows or purge records. The export scope\nis a delegation opt-in, not a replacement for the owner role: demo-reader has\nexport scope to let the host show archive availability without granting execution.\nBoth account.export and the account.archive capability perform complete exports.\nCapability metadata in contracts.py is the authoritative operation-role contract.\n\nAn authenticated reader controls capability, tenant and key arguments. Records\nnamed guide exist in both tenants; tenant names are ordinary known identifiers.\nThe service account's Store has access to both tenants, so authorization must be\ncomplete before calling it. Store deliberately applies no second policy layer.\nJobs are internal values constructed by prepare, never directly accepted by the\nregistry. No separate signed capability, job queue, approval or tenant gateway\nexists. Account rows and document bodies in seed.py are synthetic private data.\n\nThe default synthetic sessions are demo-reader, demo-owner and demo-paused.\nA caller knows its own session token; no victim token or owner access is assumed.\nUnknown tools and disabled sessions stop at the transport boundary. Direct read\nand purge routes have explicit tenant checks. Legacy manifest helpers are dormant.\nThe source tests illustrate local contracts and may be read without running them.\nThe imported third-party note is data, has no authority, and is not loaded at boot.\nReview conclusions about individual controls do not certify the entire service.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NdGk1h5R3hRgrnJSM2ZGHY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
